WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:03.870
We are here to talk to you about PyTorch
and how it's a great tool

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.608
for you to scale your deep learning models
in a cheap and a fast and efficient way.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.979
So when we're scaling neural networks up,
what that means is

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:18.151
we are adding more and more layers

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.286
to your neural network.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:25.625
And what this does is it allows your model
to capture more nuance in your data

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:29.162
and allows your model to perform
more complex tasks.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.131
Well, all of this doesn't come for free.

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:36.436
The model will start to require
more memory and more compute.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.972
Let's see the llama model, for example.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.975
It has four variants

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.177
ranging from 7 billion parameters

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.680
to 70 billion parameters.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.749
The smallest variant, 7 billion,

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.219
was trained using 2 trillion tokens

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.790
and it required around

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.993
roughly 200,000 GPU hours.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.496
That's a long time.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.932
So PyTorch, along with offering

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:09.235
the modular building blocks
to build your neural networks,

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.739
it also offers some utilities
to distribute to this training workload.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.742
And let's take a look at one of them

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.511
called Distributed Data Parallel (DDP).

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:19.579
So when you're training your model

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:22.115
with DDP, there are three steps that need to happen.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:27.620
First, the forward pass
where you take the data

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:30.457
and you pass it through the model,

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.625
computes the loss

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.161
which is then back propagated

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:39.165
through the model,
which then gives you the gradients.

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:44.104
The third step where we update the model's
weights is preceded

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:52.345
by a synchronization,
where all the computed gradients

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:57.050
from each of these replicas
are communicated with each other.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:01.588
Now, the hallmark of DDP
and really all distributed training

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.657
is an overlap of

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:08.628
the computation and communication.

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:09.796
Essentially what

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:13.933
that means is simultaneously
we are doing the back propagation

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.203
while communicating
all of the calculated gradients.

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.206
This saves us time
and keeps the GPU running

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:23.076
at a near 100% utilization.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.011
At a very high level,

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.213
what that looks like:

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:31.951
You first divide the model into buckets.

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:35.388
Each replica calculates

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.258
the gradients of the first bucket

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.660
and while it is calculating

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.330
the gradients of the second bucket,

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.835
these first bucket
gradients are synchronized.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.772
And this is happening simultaneously

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:56.142
with the computation of the gradients
in the second bucket.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:03:00.580
And similarly,
as each bucket is being calculated,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.316
the preceding buckets
gradients are being communicated.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.353
This ensures that all the GPUs are running

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:11.291
at full utilization
and you're not having any idle workers.

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.493
They're all working
very hard to train your model.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:15.728
And this is the

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:18.531
case where the model fits in one GPU.

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:22.001
For example,
the 7 billion model can fit on

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.039
on any cloud GPUs today.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:29.576
But when you start scaling
that up, like, for example, 70 billion

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:34.514
or even the 30 billion models, it's
very difficult to fit them in one GPU.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.617
So in that paradigm, in those regimes,

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:41.087
the DDP model does not work.

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:46.092
Now I'll call upon Raghu to talk to us
about what in PyTorch allows us

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:50.430
to train models which are larger than what
your single GPU can accommodate.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.466
All right, so you've heard

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:57.971
all about distributed data parallel,
and how do you scale a small model

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:01.808
on very large number of GPUs
and reduce your training times.

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:04.510
So now let's look at what happens

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.880
when we have a model
that does not fit in a single GPU.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.284
And that is
where FSDP comes to your rescue.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:15.588
FSDP stands for "Fully Sharded Data Parallel"

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:18.625
and what fully sharded
data parallel does is it

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:21.661
takes the model, breaks it down into
what are called

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:27.467
units, and all of these units are

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:31.170
then sharded across GPUs.

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:32.171
So you can take that--

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:37.243
Think of it as shredding the model
and then each GPU owns

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:40.647
small portions of this model.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.049
So that's where
shards are coming in.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:44.917
Now, what

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:48.921
happens after that is pretty much
very much like what DDP is doing.

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:53.026
Think of instead of a model,
you are thinking of it as a unit.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:56.996
And during the initial unit

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.098
construction, from the shards,

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:03.336
you are going to do an AllGather.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.739
And this happens in the forward pass.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:10.076
So you're gathering
the unit, you're computing on top of it.

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.680
And this happens across all the units.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.351
So as soon as your unit is computed,
you lose that unit's memory

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:20.353
and then you go to the next unit
and so on.

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:22.322
And that's the forward pass.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:23.856
Once the entire forward pass is

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:25.525
computed, your loss is 

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.593
computed, and you go--

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:29.829
So this is your forward pass.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:32.765
And now you're going to do an AllGather

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:36.135
in the backward pass.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:40.540
And during the backward pass,
what is happening is you are computing

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.110
again, very much like here,
you are gathering the unit, you're computing

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:47.847
the back propagation,
but just in the reverse fashion.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:51.117
Once you have computed the gradients,

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:54.687
then those are again,

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.990
very much
like what you have done for your DDP.

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:03.629
Those are synchronized across all the GPUs
that are responsible for holding

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.899
that particular portion of the model.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.269
So once you are synchronized,
that completes your entire step

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.742
and then you continue doing all of this
and then FSDP, very much like DDP,

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:19.612
you are going to utilize overlap
in a significant way

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:25.118
because imagine in DDP there was only one
single synchronization step, in FSDP,

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:30.089
I have more opportunities
for doing overlap and keeping those GPUs

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:33.960
continuously busy
while you are doing your computation.

00:06:35.763 --> 00:06:38.998
And that is how you achieve scale of these models.

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:43.636
So what are the typical ways
in which people train these large models?

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:46.939
Most of the world knows about them
as HPC systems,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:50.943
very large scale systems
with very high speed interconnects,

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:54.747
state of the art GPUs and servers
and training these models.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:07:01.287
So what happens if I have "not a HPC
system" that, you know, I have a good node.

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:05.091
So let's say that instead of this GPU,
we call this a node.

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:08.327
Typically nodes have eight,

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:11.197
maybe 16 GPUs in them.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:15.535
And many of these, the typical HPC system

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:18.905
has an HPC interconnect things like

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.443
the interconnect

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:25.378
where people

00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:27.980
may have heard the term InfiniBand.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:31.150
So very high speed
interconnect between nodes;

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:35.254
and people may
have heard the term Ethernet.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:37.924
Ethernet is far more

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:41.828
common in many environments,
whether it is cloud

00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:45.331
or on-prem environments,
whereas InfiniBand is less common.

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:50.136
So there is a common misconception
that these larger models

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:53.406
will always need InfiniBand,
even though that is true

00:07:53.406 --> 00:07:56.042
for some of the larger models
and it is faster.

00:07:56.309 --> 00:08:01.013
But with Ethernet also, you can train
these models in an efficient manner.

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:06.419
And that is where IBM helped, worked
with the PyTorch community,

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:10.857
and introduced this concept of rate
limiter.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:16.662
What rate limiter is doing,
is really balancing off the trade off

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:21.601
between the overlap
in terms of communication and computation

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:25.738
by managing the memory in the GPU better.

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.574
And that is how you reduce the amount

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:31.310
of communication per

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:35.147
GPU computation time step.
And you are increasing the amount

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:39.051
of compute while you're keeping
that communication constant.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:43.523
And that is how you can get away
with training on Ethernet

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:47.560
while achieving
similar kinds of benefits as InfiniBand.

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:52.098
So Raghu, we saw quite a few things
about how PyTorch helps

00:08:52.298 --> 00:08:55.935
in scaling up your deep
learning workloads and you AI workloads.

00:08:55.935 --> 00:08:59.605
We saw FSDP,
we saw the rate limiter API.

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:02.441
And they do a pretty good job.

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:03.609
Absolutely.

00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:05.411
And we also saw how

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:09.582
interconnects play a role on
how do you scale up and so on, right?

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:14.587
But what happens inside a single node
between that CPU and GPU?

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.722
Tell me a little bit more about it. 

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.291
Yeah, you know,

00:09:18.291 --> 00:09:21.727
it's an interesting question
you raise because there is

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:25.665
some more efficiency gains that we can
we can opt in.

00:09:26.165 --> 00:09:29.335
And let's take a look at, you know,
what's what's happening on the board.

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:32.972
So the reason-- one of the reasons --why PyTorch is

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:35.741
so popular
is because of its programing paradigm

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:37.977
called "eager mode".

00:09:37.977 --> 00:09:41.280
And eager mode allows developers

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:44.750
to have a very Pythonic approach
to their programs.

00:09:45.284 --> 00:09:48.554
It allows dynamism, like,
you know, you can do your if-else blocks.

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:51.390
You're going to have for-loops
within that.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.491
It's very flexible.

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:52.825
Yeah.

00:09:52.825 --> 00:09:55.728
And we already spoke about CPU and GPU.

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:56.728
Yeah.

00:09:57.063 --> 00:10:00.499
So I'm going to draw a
small little schematic over here,

00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:03.536
which sort of illustrates
what's happening in

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:08.941
eager mode.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:11.010
Let's say this is your CPU queue

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:15.214
and this is your GPU

00:10:15.214 --> 00:10:17.583
queue. And queue is essentially

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:23.122
the order of instructions
that each chip is launching.

00:10:23.456 --> 00:10:24.123
Mm hmm.

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:27.193
Now, taking a step back, your AI programs,

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:30.997
your AI models are essentially
a sequence of operations.

00:10:31.631 --> 00:10:37.536
Small AI models, small deep networks
have lesser instructions comparatively.

00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:40.072
And larger models have many more.

00:10:40.106 --> 00:10:41.641
Mm hmm. Mm hmm.

00:10:41.641 --> 00:10:44.043
So every line of code that I write in

00:10:44.076 --> 00:10:48.447
in my Python program
basically translates to going to the GPU.

00:10:48.514 --> 00:10:51.183
Is that essentially what's happening? Yes.

00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:54.820
Almost every line is an independent instruction

00:10:54.954 --> 00:10:58.491
that your CPU
is deriving from the program.

00:10:59.525 --> 00:11:03.295
And so we can probably think
of your model,

00:11:03.629 --> 00:11:07.533
the execution of your model,
as a sequence of instructions over here.

00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:10.503
Mm hmm.

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:13.272
And because you're using
a hardware accelerator like a GPU,

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:16.142
you're going to queue up
those instructions

00:11:16.275 --> 00:11:20.146
onto the relevant operations
specific to the back end that are using.

00:11:20.179 --> 00:11:20.813
Okay.

00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:23.816
So let's say you have CUDA operations

00:11:23.816 --> 00:11:33.125
on an Nvidia GPU.

00:11:35.294 --> 00:11:37.229
So this is kind of how eager mode works.

00:11:37.229 --> 00:11:40.966
This is the appropriate paradigm
that allows you to iterate

00:11:40.966 --> 00:11:44.670
or interact with your program
in a very, you know, 1-to-1 way.

00:11:44.737 --> 00:11:45.638
Okay.

00:11:45.771 --> 00:11:48.240
So what is happening between,

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.244
you know, these times
when-- what happens there?

00:11:52.445 --> 00:11:54.780
And that's, you know, if you've hit the

00:11:55.915 --> 00:11:58.184
nail on the head,

00:11:58.984 --> 00:12:01.654
these empty spaces over here

00:12:01.654 --> 00:12:04.256
are actually times
when the GPU is not working.

00:12:04.924 --> 00:12:06.192
These are

00:12:06.592 --> 00:12:07.426
idle.

00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:10.029
And, you know, that's what we do

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:13.833
not like about our GPU is
because they're a very expensive resource.

00:12:14.166 --> 00:12:15.568
We don't like them to be idle.

00:12:15.568 --> 00:12:18.270
We want them to be working all through,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.906
like you want to maximize the utilization over there.

00:12:21.607 --> 00:12:24.243
And these get more and more as you scale.

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:26.445
These are the model size, right? Exactly.

00:12:26.445 --> 00:12:30.750
Like as you as your models get larger,
the skew starts getting much longer.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:34.820
And as the queue starts getting longer,
the number of these idle spaces

00:12:35.387 --> 00:12:36.555
start increasing

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.491
quite a lot.

00:12:38.491 --> 00:12:38.791
Yeah.

00:12:38.791 --> 00:12:42.928
And these essentially translate
to a lot of costs, unnecessary costs.

00:12:43.262 --> 00:12:46.599
You're burning GPU hours without
actually getting any bang for your buck.

00:12:46.832 --> 00:12:49.802
Okay, so how do we address this?

00:12:49.802 --> 00:12:52.538
So here we have an interesting tradeoff.

00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:54.774
It poses a pretty interesting
engineering challenge.

00:12:55.207 --> 00:12:57.643
We want that you got more, like, flexibility.

00:12:58.177 --> 00:13:00.479
It makes programing fun. Mm hmm.

00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:02.481
But we also want that efficiency. Mm hmm.

00:13:02.715 --> 00:13:05.151
So earlier last last year

00:13:05.951 --> 00:13:10.256
at the PyTorch conference,
we announced 2.0.

00:13:10.256 --> 00:13:15.327
So PyTorch 2.0 packs
in a very interesting new paradigm.

00:13:15.728 --> 00:13:18.330
So it's essentially a new compiler

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.699
called TorchDynamo.

00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:22.668
And this still allows you

00:13:22.668 --> 00:13:25.971
to program your PyTorch code,
as you always have.

00:13:26.405 --> 00:13:29.141
It's still got that flexibility,
that interactivity.

00:13:30.276 --> 00:13:35.447
But what it does
differently is instead of having

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.819
separate instructions

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.721
queued up like in eager mode,

00:13:42.721 --> 00:13:46.892
your program is essentially converted
to a graph of operations.

00:13:47.293 --> 00:13:48.794
It's almost a static graph.

00:13:48.794 --> 00:13:51.630
And so all of these instructions
are sort of merged together.

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:52.231
Mm hmm.

00:13:52.898 --> 00:13:56.902
And they form like it's a 1-to-m.

00:13:57.336 --> 00:13:57.937
Mm hmm.

00:13:57.937 --> 00:13:59.939
And sometimes you might have graph breaks,
You know,

00:13:59.939 --> 00:14:03.409
you might want to come back to eager
mode paradigm.

00:14:03.909 --> 00:14:14.820
So your entire program
has been translated

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:19.925
to two instructions over here instead of many different instructions.

00:14:22.561 --> 00:14:24.663
And on the GPU,

00:14:25.264 --> 00:14:28.234
again,
you're queuing up only two instructions

00:14:28.234 --> 00:14:30.870
instead of all of n instructions
over here.

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.138
Yeah.

00:14:35.641 --> 00:14:38.177
And quite often
you won't even have this break.

00:14:38.444 --> 00:14:42.781
So your entire program, your entire model,
is this one block of instructions in it

00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:46.151
which executes seamlessly on the GPU.

00:14:46.285 --> 00:14:47.119
That is pretty cool.

00:14:47.119 --> 00:14:51.757
And how do you actually achieve this
from a programing standpoint?

00:14:51.757 --> 00:14:54.960
Like what does, say me, as a developer,

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.363
what do I need to do to get this?

00:14:58.931 --> 00:15:00.266
One line of code.

00:15:00.266 --> 00:15:04.036
That's wonderful.

00:15:06.438 --> 00:15:06.972
All of this

00:15:06.972 --> 00:15:10.109
goodness
in one API called torch.compile.

00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:13.679
So that's like super easy for me
as a developer to get up to.

00:15:13.679 --> 00:15:16.982
What kind of speed ups
have you seen with torch.compile?

00:15:16.982 --> 00:15:19.184
Let's say just about training it.

00:15:20.052 --> 00:15:21.854
Oh, torch.compile

00:15:21.854 --> 00:15:23.889
primarily targets training workloads

00:15:24.356 --> 00:15:28.360
and depending on the architecture
and the model, it varies.

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:31.830
But we have seen speed ups of at least
40 times. Wow.

00:15:32.197 --> 00:15:34.233
So that is an order of magnitude.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:38.771
Many orders of magnitude of on display
and eager mode.

00:15:39.004 --> 00:15:40.272
Okay, wonderful.

